  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1234 AM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nWeak zonal flow aloft across North-Montana and weak southerly flow \nover SW Montana will keep conditions relatively quiet on Tuesday, \nwith temperatures cooling to near-seasonal averages. Isolated, light \nshowers remain possible near the Idaho border, but dry conditions \nwill prevail everywhere else. \n\nOn Wednesday, an upper-level low settles along the California coast, \nincreasing our southwesterly flow aloft over SW Montana while zonal \nflow persists across North-Central MT. Strengthened warm air \nadvection with the southwesterly regime will rebound high \ntemperatures back to well above seasonal averages. Mostly dry \nconditions will continue across the plains, though weak moisture \nadvection supports a low chance for a shower or two along the ID/MT \nborder. \n\nAn active synoptic pattern arrives late in the week, as the pacific \nlow pressure eject inland, and begins to phase with the digging \ntrough emerging out of Canada. A weak cold front will pass through \nthe region Thursday night into Friday morning, helping initiate \nwidespread precipitation that persists through early Saturday.\nThis system will be fairly heavy in moisture with high confidence in \na large area seeing over 0.5\" of liquid precipitation. An axis of \nheavier amounts is likely across Southwest and Central Montana where \nareas could see over 1\" of QPF. Precipitation will begin with high \nsnow level Thursday night (Above 6kft). During the heaviest period \nof precip on Friday morning, diabatic and dynamic cooling will help \npull levels down towards 5kft, transitioning much of the mountain \nran to accumulation snow. As the primary deformation axis and \nmoisture depart Friday night into Saturday, additional cold air \nadvects in lowering snow levels below 4kft. However, mid-level \ndrying will quick cut off precipitation rates, limiting low-\nelevation accumulation potential. Simultaneously, a tightening \nsurface pressure gradient and increasing low-level lapse rates will \nhelp tap the stronger 700mb flow on Friday bringing gusty winds \nacross the region.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nConfidence is moderate to high regarding elevated post-frontal winds \non Friday. The probability for gusts reaching or exceeding 45mph is \n40-60% across the Hi-Line and central Plains, across the RMF and \ncentral Island ranges that rises up to 50-70%. \n\nFor snowfall, confidence is medium for accumulation snowfall above \n4kft, with a 35-55% chance of exceeding 2 inches especially across \nthe RMF and central Island ranges. For elevations below 4kft, the \nprobability of exceeding 1\" is largely under 10%. The main question \nmark for the weekend storm centers on how long moisture wants to \nhang around as snow level drop further especially with the phasing \nof the low and trough.\n  -Scenario 1: The low phases and swings through more progressively, \nmoisture exits rapidly before snow levels fall below 4kft, confining \naccumulation snow to the mountains and passes while valley floors \nsee rain ended as non accumulation \"mood-flakes\" with no impacts.\n -Scenario 2: The low/trough phasing is slower, both delaying the\nprogression of the troughing but also allowing deformation \nmoisture to linger across the area while cold air drives snow \nlevels down towards 3kft. This will introduce a window for a more \npersistent but light snowfall across lower elevations. That being \nsaid, warm ground temperatures and hours of rain proceeding the \npotential change would restrict any of this to elevated surface if\nany at all. \n-JR",
